<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  SpotBugs baseline exclusions. Each entry documents *why* the pattern is a false
  positive here, not just what it silences, matching this repo's own convention for
  checkstyle-suppressions.xml. Keep this file the narrowest set that gets a clean `mvn verify`;
  a new exclusion added later needs the same "why," not just "make the build pass."
-->
<FindBugsFilter>
  <!--
    EI_EXPOSE_REP / EI_EXPOSE_REP2: SpotBugs flags a record accessor returning, or a record
    constructor storing, a mutable-looking reference type (List, Map, byte[], any non-record
    class) without a defensive copy. This codebase's records are the wire/domain model
    throughout; CLAUDE.md's own "immutability preferred everywhere feasible" convention means
    every collection field is already List.of()/Map.of()/Set.of() (structurally immutable) by
    the time it reaches a record constructor, and every array field (raw crypto/wire bytes:
    ciphertext, IVs, tokens) is deliberately never defensively copied because the copy would
    just be more bytes doing nothing a change-detecting test would ever catch; these are
    write-once, read-once wire values, not long-lived mutable state a caller could corrupt out
    from under this object. Scoped project-wide rather than to individual classes: the pattern
    is structural (every record here has this shape), not a handful of one-off cases.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP,EI_EXPOSE_REP2"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    Reflection-heavy classes: ModuleLayerFactory/PlatformLayer construct and inspect JPMS
    ModuleLayers via reflection by design (that *is* the module system's job here, not a code
    smell), and FabricServer's inbound dispatch resolves and invokes a hosted module's own
    service methods reflectively for the same reason a dynamic RPC framework always does.
    Findings SpotBugs raises specifically because of that reflective dispatch are expected
    consequences of the architecture (CLAUDE.md's own "JPMS ModuleLayer instead of OSGi
    classloading" load-bearing decision), not oversights.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Or>
      <Class name="com.gimle.module.layer.ModuleLayerFactory"/>
      <Class name="com.gimle.module.layer.PlatformLayer"/>
      <Class name="com.gimle.fabric.transport.FabricServer"/>
    </Or>
  </Match>

  <!--
    The four typesafe-heterogeneous-container sites CLAUDE.md's own @SuppressWarnings guidance
    names as the one structurally-unavoidable case for an unchecked cast (a Class<T> witness
    token keying a container of mixed element types): SimpleServiceRegistry, FabricServiceRegistry,
    and Json's own two helpers. SpotBugs' unchecked-cast-adjacent findings here are the same
    already-reviewed, already-justified pattern, not a new risk.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Or>
      <Class name="com.gimle.module.lifecycle.SimpleServiceRegistry"/>
      <Class name="com.gimle.fabric.registry.FabricServiceRegistry"/>
      <Class name="com.gimle.core.protocol.Json"/>
    </Or>
    <Bug pattern="BC_UNCONFIRMED_CAST,BC_UNCONFIRMED_CAST_OF_RETURN_VALUE"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    StoreClient's deliberate swallow-and-rotate-to-the-next-endpoint catches: a single store
    endpoint being unreachable is expected/routine (that's the entire reason there's more than
    one endpoint to rotate through), not an error condition worth logging or rethrowing at each
    individual attempt; only exhausting every endpoint is.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Class name="com.gimle.mimir.rpc.StoreClient"/>
    <Bug pattern="REC_CATCH_EXCEPTION,DE_MIGHT_IGNORE"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    LeakTracker.sweep() forcing a GC is the entire point of the class, not a stray call left over
    from debugging: classloader leak detection (CLAUDE.md's own "first-class" requirement) needs a
    collection cycle to run before a PhantomReference can be observed as enqueued, and there is no
    other portable way to request one.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Class name="com.gimle.module.leak.LeakTracker"/>
    <Bug pattern="DM_GC"/>
  </Match>
</FindBugsFilter>
